Geoapify | How to Plan a Bicycle Route with Bike Type, Surface, and Elevation | Geoapify > Add elevation, climb, and descent

The complete leg has 1,229 entries in elevation_range and elevation , one for each point of the leg geometry. The example shows the first four and the first of 62 steps.

| Field | Level | Meaning |
| elevation_range | leg | [distance, height] pairs in meters. Distance is measured along the leg from its start. |
| elevation | leg | Heights in meters for the leg geometry points |
| max_elevation , min_elevation | step | Highest and lowest point of the step |
| elevation_gain | step | Height of the last point of the step minus height of the first point. Negative values mean the step goes downhill. |
| elevation | step | Average elevation of the step |

The response has no total ascent or descent field. Calculate them from elevation_range :

```
function summarizeElevation(routingResponse) {
  let ascent = 0;
  let descent = 0;
  let min = Infinity;
  let max = -Infinity;

for (const leg of routingResponse.results[0].legs) {
    const range = leg.elevation_range ?? [];

range.forEach(([, height], i) => {
      min = Math.min(min, height);
      max = Math.max(max, height);
      if (i === 0) return;

// Add every rise to the ascent and every drop to the descent.
      const change = height - range[i - 1][1];
      if (change > 0) ascent += change;
      else descent -= change;
    });
  }

return {
    ascent: Math.round(ascent),
    descent: Math.round(descent),
    min: Math.round(min),
    max: Math.round(max)
  };
}
```

