Geoapify | Google OVER_QUERY_LIMIT: Causes and Fixes | Geoapify > Retry with backoff

Even with pacing, a request can still hit the limit when other clients share the project. Retrying with exponential backoff means waiting longer after each failed attempt (here 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 seconds), so retries don't add to the burst that caused the error:

```
const addresses = [... /* your addresses here */];

const geocoder = new google.maps.Geocoder();
const baseDelay = 1000; // first retry after 1 s
const maxRetryCount = 5;

geocodeAddresses(addresses, geocoder);

function wait(ms) {
  return new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms));
}

function geocodeAddress(address, geocoder, attempt = 0) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    geocoder.geocode({ address: address }, (results, status) => {
      if (status === 'OK') {
        resolve(results[0]);
      } else if (status === 'OVER_QUERY_LIMIT' && attempt < maxRetryCount) {
        // Exponential backoff: 1 s, 2 s, 4 s, 8 s, 16 s
        const delay = baseDelay * 2 ** attempt;
        wait(delay)
          .then(() => geocodeAddress(address, geocoder, attempt + 1))
          .then(resolve)
          .catch(reject);
      } else if (status === 'OVER_QUERY_LIMIT') {
        reject(new Error(`Geocoding failed after ${maxRetryCount} retries: ${address}`));
      } else {
        reject(new Error(`Geocoding failed: ${address} (${status})`));
      }
    });
  });
}

Geoapify | Google OVER_QUERY_LIMIT: Causes and Fixes | Geoapify > Retry with backoff

async function geocodeAddresses(addresses, geocoder) {
  const results = [];
  for (const address of addresses) {
    try {
      const result = await geocodeAddress(address, geocoder);
      results.push(result);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error.message);
    }
  }
  console.log(results);
}
```

The geocodeAddress function takes one address, the Geocoder instance and the attempt number, and returns a Promise:

- If the status is OK , it resolves with the first result.

- If the status is OVER_QUERY_LIMIT , it waits baseDelay * 2 ** attempt milliseconds and tries again, up to maxRetryCount retries.

- Any other status (for example ZERO_RESULTS or REQUEST_DENIED ) rejects immediately, because retrying won't help.

geocodeAddresses processes addresses one at a time, so only one request is in flight. Retries handle short spikes well; if the error persists after all retries, the cause is more likely a quota or billing limit than the per-minute rate.

